[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified the OSPF stub area behind R2 (Area 4 on 21.1.1.0/24, R2-eth2 to R10-eth0). The ABR is R2 (router-id 23.1.1.1) and R10 is an internal area router (router-id 110.1.4.1). In a stub area, the ABR should inject a default route and flood inter-area summary LSAs; internal routers should not originate defaults. That is exactly what we see:

- On R10, OSPF database shows a single default summary LSA 0.0.0.0/0, Advertising Router 23.1.1.1 (R2), metric 1 (evidence: “show ip ospf database summary” on R10). R10’s routing table has O>* 0.0.0.0/0 via 21.1.1.1 (R2), and no competing default.
- On R2, there is no 0.0.0.0/0 learned back from R10; R2 uses specific inter-area routes into area 0 and intra-area routes into area 4 (evidence: “show ip route” on R2 shows 100.0.0.0/8 and 110.1.x.x via 21.1.1.2, but no default via area 4).
- End-to-end forwarding is healthy: pings from R10 to PC0 and PC12 succeed with 0% loss; PCs can reach each other normally.

Mechanism: You are seeing many summary LSAs inside Area 4 because it is configured as a regular stub (not totally-stubby); this is expected and not a “summarization loop.” Localization: Area 4 between R2 (eth2) and R10 (eth0). Root cause: No mis-summarization or bad default is present. Corrective fix: None required. If the goal is to reduce inter-area LSAs in Area 4, optionally set “area 4 stub no-summary” on R2 (ABR).
=== END ===